<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arabs are a people originating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> who speak Arabic. With the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the seventh century, Arab armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> and Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> and much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Arab merchants and settlers later spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, influencing states from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Perhaps 100,000 <b>Arabs</b> then lived in Ifriqiya, most of them immigrants from Syria and Iraq, but the Berbers remained the great majority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Caucasus the Alans controlled the Darial Pass and held the balance between Byzantium, Persia, the Khazars and later the <b>Arabs</b>, and their successor, the Kingdom of Alania, adopted Christianity in the early tenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arabs</b> from the Eastern Desert settled the Butana and the Gezira, and by about 1500 the Nubians had no king but 150 lordships centred on castles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around and beneath them lived settled farmers and townspeople: Kurds, Armenians, Syriac Christians, <b>Arabs</b> and, after the conquests of the 1460s, the Persian-speaking population of the Iranian plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nicephorus II Phocas retook Cilicia from the <b>Arabs</b> in 965 the Byzantines settled Christians from Syria and Armenia there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest military asset was Constantinople itself: its walls withstood repeated sieges over eight centuries — by Avars and Persians in 626, <b>Arabs</b> in 674–678 and 717–718, Bulgarians and Rus' — and each failure of an attacker bought the empire more time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
